      "side": 1,
      "message": "\"android-non-updatable\" is NOT a java_sdk_library. In fact it is not a module at all. However, there are \"android-non-updatable.stubs\" and \"android-non-updatable.system.stubs\", etc. which is what an \"android-non-updatable\" java_sdk_library would create.\n\nSo, instead of having nearly every mainline module specify the following in their bootclasspath_fragment {\n  ...\n  public: {\n    additional_stubs: [\"android-non-updatable.stubs\"],\n  }\n  system: {\n    additional_stubs: [\"android-non-updatable.system.stubs\"],\n  }\n  test: {\n    additional_stubs: [\"android-non-updatable.test.stubs\"],\n  }\n  module_lib: {\n    additional_stubs: [\"android-non-updatable.module_lib.stubs\"],\n  }\n  ...\n}\n\nThey can just specify:\n  \nbootclasspath_fragment {\n  ...\n  additional_stubs: [\"android-non-updatable\"],\n  ...\n}\n\nAnd have it behave as if they had done the first (if it actually worked).\n\nNow, if http://b/192067200 is possible then we can replace all those separate java_library modules with a single java_sdk_library compatible module called \"android-non-updatable\". We can just remove the special treatment from Soong and it will all work fine.",
